### What is the N+1 problem in Hibernate?

The classic Hibernate N+1 happens when you fetch a list of entities and then access a lazily loaded association for each one. Fetching 100 accounts issues one SELECT, and touching each account's transactions issues one SELECT per account, giving 101 queries.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import java.util.List;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    // WRONG: triggers N+1 when transactions are lazily loaded
    List<Account> findAll();

    // CORRECT: JOIN FETCH loads transactions in one query
    @Query("SELECT a FROM Account a JOIN FETCH a.transactions WHERE a.branch = :branch")
    List<Account> findAllWithTransactions(String branch);
}
```

```text
// WRONG (N+1):
// Query 1: SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE branch = 'NBI-DELHI' → 100 rows
// Query 2: SELECT * FROM transactions WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
// Query 3: SELECT * FROM transactions WHERE account_id = 'SAV2002'
// ... 100 more queries
// Total: 101 queries, ~100ms

// CORRECT (JOIN FETCH):
// Query 1: SELECT a.*, t.* FROM accounts a JOIN transactions t ... → all data
// Total: 1 query, ~5ms
```

### Why is the network version worse than the database version?

In microservices, the +N calls become network round trips to other services. A database round trip is roughly 1ms, so 101 queries take about 100ms. A network call to another service is 20-100ms, so 101 network calls take 2-10 seconds. Each network call is also an independent chance for a timeout, dropped connection, or circuit breaker trip.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.client.BalanceClient;
import com.bank.model.AccountSummary;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class RelationshipManagerDashboard {

    private final BalanceClient balanceClient;

    public RelationshipManagerDashboard(BalanceClient balanceClient) {
        this.balanceClient = balanceClient;
    }

    // WRONG: N+1 across the network
    public List<AccountSummary> buildReport(List<String> accountIds) {
        return accountIds.stream()
                .map(id -> {
                    // Each call: ~30ms network round trip
                    BigDecimal balance = balanceClient.getBalance(id);
                    return new AccountSummary(id, balance);
                })
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

```text
// 100 accounts x 30ms per call = 3000ms (3 seconds!)
// Plus: each call can independently timeout or fail
// If call #47 times out → entire dashboard fails or shows partial data
```

### How do you fix it with a batch endpoint?

The best fix is a bulk endpoint that accepts a list of IDs and returns all results in one call. This collapses 1+N into 1+1.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.service.AccountService;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/accounts")
public class AccountController {

    private final AccountService accountService;

    public AccountController(AccountService accountService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
    }

    // Batch endpoint: accepts multiple IDs, returns all balances
    @PostMapping("/batch/balances")
    public Map<String, BigDecimal> getBatchBalances(
            @RequestBody List<String> accountIds) {
        return accountService.getBalances(accountIds);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.client.BalanceClient;
import com.bank.model.AccountSummary;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class RelationshipManagerDashboardFixed {

    private final BalanceClient balanceClient;

    public RelationshipManagerDashboardFixed(BalanceClient balanceClient) {
        this.balanceClient = balanceClient;
    }

    // CORRECT: one batch call instead of N calls
    public List<AccountSummary> buildReport(List<String> accountIds) {
        Map<String, BigDecimal> balances = balanceClient
                .getBatchBalances(accountIds); // 1 call for all 100
        return accountIds.stream()
                .map(id -> new AccountSummary(id, balances.get(id)))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

```text
// Before: 1 + 100 network calls = ~3000ms
// After:  1 + 1 network call    = ~35ms
// Speedup: ~85x faster
```

### How do you fix it with caching?

When balances change slowly, cache them in Redis. Use `multiGet` to fetch many at once.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class CachedBalanceService {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public CachedBalanceService(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
    }

    public Map<String, BigDecimal> getCachedBalances(List<String> accountIds) {
        List<String> keys = accountIds.stream()
                .map(id -> "balance:" + id)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        // One Redis call for all 100 keys
        List<String> values = redis.opsForValue().multiGet(keys);

        return IntStream.range(0, accountIds.size())
                .boxed()
                .filter(i -> values != null && values.get(i) != null)
                .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                        accountIds::get,
                        i -> new BigDecimal(values.get(i))));
    }
}
```

```text
// Redis multiGet for 100 keys: ~2ms (single round trip)
// vs 100 individual GET calls: ~100ms
// Cache miss: fall back to batch endpoint, then populate cache
```

### How do you fix it with parallel fan-out?

When you truly cannot batch, fire the N calls concurrently but cap the concurrency to avoid overwhelming downstream.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.client.BalanceClient;
import com.bank.model.AccountSummary;
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class ParallelBalanceFetcher {

    private final BalanceClient balanceClient;

    public ParallelBalanceFetcher(BalanceClient balanceClient) {
        this.balanceClient = balanceClient;
    }

    public List<AccountSummary> fetchWithBoundedConcurrency(
            List<String> accountIds) {
        return Flux.fromIterable(accountIds)
                .flatMap(id -> Mono.fromCallable(
                        () -> new AccountSummary(id, balanceClient.getBalance(id))),
                        10) // Max 10 concurrent calls
                .collectList()
                .block();
    }
}
```

```text
// Sequential: 100 calls x 30ms = 3000ms
// Parallel (bounded at 10): 10 batches x 30ms = ~300ms
// Parallel (unbounded): 100 simultaneous calls → overwhelms downstream!
// Bounded concurrency is the safe middle ground
```

### What are common mistakes? (wrong vs correct)

The most dangerous mistake is unbounded parallelism, which turns N+1 into a denial-of-service attack on your own downstream service.

```java
// WRONG: unbounded parallelism
accountIds.parallelStream()
        .map(id -> balanceClient.getBalance(id)) // All 100 at once!
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
// This floods the Balance Service with 100 simultaneous requests
// It may crash the downstream or exhaust its connection pool

// CORRECT: bounded concurrency with Flux.flatMap(mapper, concurrency)
Flux.fromIterable(accountIds)
        .flatMap(id -> fetchBalance(id), 10) // Max 10 at a time
        .collectList();
```

### How does batching change failure semantics?

With N separate calls, one failure affects one item. With one batch call, one failure could affect all items. Design for partial results with per-item status.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record BatchBalanceResult(
        String accountId,
        BigDecimal balance,
        String status  // "OK" or "ERROR"
) {}

// Batch response with per-item status:
// [
//   {accountId: "SAV2001", balance: 75000, status: "OK"},
//   {accountId: "SAV2002", balance: null, status: "ERROR"},
//   {accountId: "SAV2003", balance: 32000, status: "OK"}
// ]
// The dashboard shows balance for SAV2001 and SAV2003,
// and shows "unavailable" for SAV2002
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer always designs batch endpoints when building microservices. They also know that the N+1 pattern can appear in any layer: database, service-to-service, and even at the API gateway aggregation level. The fix at every layer is the same: collapse many individual calls into fewer, larger calls.

```java
// Senior checklist for N+1 prevention:
// 1. Every service exposes batch/bulk endpoints alongside single-item ones
// 2. Batch endpoints return per-item status, not all-or-nothing
// 3. Cache slowly-changing data with Redis multiGet
// 4. When batching is impossible, use bounded parallel fan-out
// 5. Monitor for sequential remote calls in loops (code review signal)
// 6. Set connection pool limits to prevent downstream flooding
```
